course: dictionary
lesson: 10150
title: Throughout: every part, or all the time
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Throughout tells you where or when something happens. It means the whole of a place, or the whole of a time, from start to end. Look at the words after it to decide which one it means. «The anaesthetist watched the patient throughout the operation.» The operation is a length of time, so the watching lasted from the start to the end.
explain: Throughout can also mean every part of a place. «Blood flows throughout the body.» The blood does not stop in one spot. It goes to every part of you. Try "all the way through" or "in every part of" in the sentence to check.
explain: Throughout can be a preposition with a noun after it, as in «throughout the week». It can also be an adverb with no noun after it, as in «The music played softly, and it stayed soft throughout.» Both uses carry the same idea: from start to end, or everywhere.
fact: Throughout means in every part of a place, or during the whole of a time.
fact: «Throughout the day» means from morning to night, all day long.
fact: «Throughout the world» means in every country.
fact: «Throughout history» means across all of the past.
fact: A quick check: swap in "during the whole of" or "in every part of" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The class read the book throughout the term.» What does throughout mean here? | *During the whole of the term, from start to end | Before the term began | In one corner of the classroom
exercise: «Blood flows throughout the body.» Where does the blood go? | Out of the body | Only to the heart | *To every part of the body
exercise: «The shop is open throughout the week.» When is the shop open? | *Every day of the week | Never | Only on Monday | Only at the weekend
test: «Mia smiled throughout the party.» What does throughout mean? | Only at the end of the party | Before the party started | *During the whole party, from start to end | Just for one minute
test: Which phrase has the same meaning as throughout in «The rain fell throughout the town»? | Just after | A little before | *In every part of | Not at all
exam: «Throughout the operation, the anaesthetist watched the patient closely.» What does throughout mean in this sentence? | *During the whole operation, from start to end | After the operation had finished | In one part of the hospital | Only for a moment at the start
